</h4>

<p>
Wow Tom. Nice piece of deadline work. The ending, as others have said, hit hard. The details -- money in a plastic bag, the smell of the house, etc. -- make the story real.<br />
<br />
Did you line up Michael beforehand, or did you run into him at the event? If you met him before, how much reporting did you do prior to Wednesday? If you met him just that day, how did meeting him change the way you approached the writing here? In other words, was this supposed to be a story about an event, but you turned it instead into a story about a guy? 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:richardlake@yahoo.com">r</a>
	

 at November 29,2007 14:26
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2905"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Doing The Math
</h4>

<p>
Thanks, guys. To answer your question, Richard, this came to me as an assignment about an event. Go cover Math Bowl. I got the assignment the day before. I tried to do some pre-reporting by calling some officials, but none of it amounted to a whole lot. I did not lay eyes on Michael until Wednesday morning, after his teacher whispered to me a few details about his story. Even then, I wasn't sure what to do: Could I use those details? What would his dad say? Would Michael be too embarrassed? So I just sort of hung out, observing the competition, taking down lots of details, watching him get his trophy, and when it was over I called my editor with three options: do a basic overview daily on the competition, do a more in-depth, longer-term story on Michael, or try to get Michael's whole story in one day. She said go for the third option, so I did. I interviewed Michael at a bowling alley, where his math team had gone to celebrate, and then I drove out to visit his dad so I could get his perspective and see how Michael lives. Then I returned to the office and proceeded to get very stuck on how to write the story, and to miss deadline by about an hour. Fortunately, the night editor is a very patient man.<br />
<br />
I sure hope this kid makes it.
</p>
